WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.383
Inter-process communication is more than a convenient way to move bytes between local processes.

2
00:00:05.383 --> 00:00:15.430
In C++ systems, IPC often connects a desktop user interface to a service, a host process to a helper, a broker to a contained worker, or two services

3
00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:16.865
owned by different teams.

4
00:00:16.865 --> 00:00:20.813
That communication can cross important trust, privilege, session, and lifecycle boundaries.

5
00:00:20.813 --> 00:00:22.966
Local does not automatically mean trusted.

6
00:00:22.966 --> 00:00:31.578
A caller may run as a different user, in a different session, with different permissions, inside a container, or at a different integrity level.

7
00:00:31.578 --> 00:00:36.960
It may also be outdated, misconfigured, interrupted, or sending messages the receiver did not expect.

8
00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:43.419
Treating every local peer as friendly makes the IPC endpoint harder to review and harder to operate safely.

9
00:00:43.419 --> 00:00:52.390
Secure IPC design starts by defining who may connect, what each caller is allowed to request, what message shapes are accepted, and how authority is

10
00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:53.826
attached to each operation.

11
00:00:53.826 --> 00:01:01.361
The server should validate messages before trusted use, check permissions close to the boundary, and avoid assuming that connection success means

12
00:01:01.361 --> 00:01:02.079
operation success.

13
00:01:02.079 --> 00:01:04.591
Failure behavior is part of the boundary.

14
00:01:04.591 --> 00:01:09.973
Peers can disconnect, send partial messages, time out, restart, or fail during a sensitive operation.

15
00:01:09.973 --> 00:01:17.868
A mature IPC design preserves invariants, releases resources, records safe diagnostics, and leaves the application in a known state when the peer

16
00:01:17.868 --> 00:01:19.662
does not behave as expected.

17
00:01:19.662 --> 00:01:26.480
Review should cover both normal message flow and the strange local conditions that appear during upgrades, restarts, and crashes.
